from write_section import *
b=[]
b.append(qa('Why are Waterloo’s co-op students so effective?', ['Grigorik credits alternating academic terms with work terms, rather than treating industry experience as an optional interruption after years of study. Roughly six placements offer repeated experiments with industry, company size, role, and working environment.', 'A short placement can disprove a preconceived preference without the resume stigma of repeatedly leaving permanent jobs. Someone who imagined Wall Street may discover the city, people, or work do not suit them. Repeated choices build a better model of personal fit.', 'The applied work also puts theoretical tools in context. An engineer must solve a real problem under constraints and recognize when a theoretically sophisticated optimization does not matter—for example, when the array has only ten items. He would like more universities to adopt that repeated learning loop.'],41.36,274.48))
b.append(qa('Why found PostRank after graduation?', ['He initially planned graduate school partly because he did not know what to do. During the summer, he explored a product idea: links could signal useful content, but comments, likes, and other social interactions could add signals in the newer web. PostRank aggregated those interactions to rank posts.', 'His launch brought both demand and implementation problems. He recalls several sleepless days because the servers struggled, admitting that poor code contributed. Publicity led to investor conversations.', 'Facing the graduate-school start, he noticed that the courses most appealing to him concerned entrepreneurship. He could study those ideas academically or learn while already speaking with investors. He postponed the program, closed funding, and never returned to complete the degree. The choice was an experiment connected to a real opportunity, rather than a predetermined founder identity.'],276.8,519.12))
b.append(qa('How did Google’s acquisition conversation begin?', 'After a conference presentation, a Google Analytics product leader approached the team and invited a conversation. The discussion was friendly and exploratory, beginning with understanding the product and asking whether they could work together. Grigorik later understood that Google’s response to Facebook and its social initiatives created demand for relevant talent and infrastructure. Analytics could use their experience to understand social engagement, making a mutually useful connection.',521.68,675.76,[follow('How did Google decide levels and compensation for an acquired team?', 'The team, including Grigorik, underwent full interview panels in an accelerated group process. Google evaluated engineering quality and level rather than assume acquisition alone established those. Deal negotiations also had distinct layers: people, customers, product, and intellectual property.',678.24,754.72),follow('Was an acquisition payment simply a large signing bonus?', 'Not necessarily. Different deals acquire different combinations of assets and people. PostRank supplied useful intellectual property, but its product largely had to be rebuilt within Google Analytics. Moving from tens of thousands of accounts to internet-scale pipelines changed the engineering economics: even a small field addition could imply enormous storage. He remembers learning from reliability engineers to scrutinize such changes.',731,843.52)]))
b.append(qa('What was your role in Google Analytics, and why leave a director trajectory?', ['He joined as an engineering manager because he had been founder and CTO. He remained committed to the original aim of helping publishers understand engagement and spent about a year rebuilding the stack. With the team on a sound trajectory, he considered another direction.', 'A VP offered a clear path toward director, but Google’s technical environment drew his curiosity. Internal documents showed work far ahead of published papers, and engineers sometimes lacked time rather than permission to share the advances. He became interested in contributing to a broader web ecosystem.', 'The Make the Web Fast group worked across cellular systems, protocols, proxies, and the fundamental question of measuring speed. Grigorik chose a lateral move into IC work even though he might be downleveled and reset the conventional management path. He valued more self-direction, fascinating problems, and collaborators he admired.'],845.44,1196.8,[follow('Were curiosity and control of your time the actual tradeoff?', 'Yes. He says a manager’s calendar largely serves other people’s needs. He wanted another mode in which he could direct more of his own work toward research or projects where his skills were especially useful. He acknowledges substantial uncertainty in making the choice.',1198.64,1235)]))
b.append(qa('Why later return to management in developer relations?', ['He regards movement between IC and manager as an underappreciated pattern among senior engineers. A person may first discover a path alone, then realize that building the full solution requires a team. His analogy distinguishes cutting a trail from constructing a highway.', 'His performance work moved into browser standards and metrics. Once those metrics existed, the bottleneck became adoption across analytics vendors and open-source projects. One person could not do all that outreach. A developer-relations team could accelerate adoption that otherwise might take several years.', 'He therefore returned to recruiting and organizing people in service of the problem. The management move was a means of carrying work through a new stage, not a permanent declaration that people management was the only kind of growth.'],1235,1472.32,[follow('Is a tour of duty about whichever role best serves the mission?', 'Yes, while not every problem must become his personal mission. Often success means helping a team reach the point where it can execute without him. Continuing dependence suggests he has not sufficiently strengthened it. If he commits to lead for a period, he still needs a succession plan: finish the problem or establish a self-sufficient trajectory, then step back and find the next problem as an IC.',1473.84,1623.04)]))
b.append(qa('What does principal-to-distinguished growth at Shopify require?', ['He does not supply a checklist or name one promotion project. A principal engineer must identify useful work amid ambiguity. He expects someone entering an unfamiliar area to learn the landscape, build partnerships, understand leaders’ problems, and determine where their skills apply.', 'For distinguished work, he emphasizes a demonstrated range across technical layers and business requirements. Someone must understand low-level constraints, reconstruct the actual problem, and connect it to what product and business partners need.', 'The execution mode also changes with the situation. A frontier problem requires rapid experiments that reveal unknowns; a long-lived platform API requires slow consideration of consequences for the ecosystem. In either case, exploration must lead to a recommendation and useful execution. He compares the role’s ambiguity with a VP receiving difficult problems that lower layers could not solve.'],1623.04,1880,[follow('Why aim to be the only person rather than the best?', ['He respects deep specialists but considers a portfolio more resilient if demand changes. Modest design, sales, support, hosting, security, and engineering skills can combine into a useful capability that otherwise requires a team. When a specialist arrives, he can hand over one part and focus elsewhere.', 'Cross-functional learning also lets him translate among marketing, PR, engineering, and business partners. He recalls intensive marketing reading to learn their vocabulary. His rapid-learning percentages are personal heuristics; they are not demonstrated learning rates for every domain.', 'The senior person need not know the most. His responsibility can be finding experts and helping them navigate the difficult space between platform possibilities and business requirements. Knowing how to find answers and facilitate decisions is different from memorizing every API.'],1880,2194.96)]))
b.append(qa('Does a broad skill intersection make a generalist distinctive?', 'He agrees. A person with sufficient knowledge of several relevant areas can combine them for an unusual problem and change their contribution as circumstances change. Management experience is another available tool: even as an IC, he can coach, give feedback, and partner effectively with managers. His probability-language analogy expresses uniqueness; the interview does not establish an independence-based mathematical model for career success.',2197.44,2304.4,[follow('Should an IC try management, and is returning a demotion?', 'He suggests team leadership as a way to explore and management itself if it suits the person. He wants organizations to permit movement in both directions without penalty. Senior managers can value what they learned while still remembering an earlier technical role as their happiest period. Returning to that kind of work can be a new challenge rather than a failure.',2306.32,2407.68)]))
b.append(qa('What regrets should others learn from?', ['Do not let success raise the threshold for trying so high that it stops learning. His early writing and code now seem embarrassing, but publishing them generated feedback and encouragement that made improvement possible.', 'A larger audience creates an expectation of exceptional output. That can suppress ordinary observations and experiments that would once have been shared freely. He continues to struggle with that filter and encourages the courage to be mistaken or disliked without claiming to know everything.'],2409.84,2569.12,[follow('How do you enter an unfamiliar domain when people expect a distinguished engineer to know the answers?', 'Set expectations explicitly. He arrives to help and learn, asks foundational questions, and explains that he needs to build a model quickly. Curiosity can otherwise sound like an attack. Acknowledging both what others know and what he can contribute makes the exchange collaborative: they supply domain expertise while he can remove obstacles or connect ideas.',2569.92,2703.6)]))
b.append(qa('What author or book influenced your career?', 'He recommends Sir Ken Robinson’s books and talks about education, creativity, and finding one’s element. The philosophy resonates with the co-op discussion: allow people to discover their strengths and combine skills rather than fit a rigid age-based factory model. He recognizes that implementing such an education system is difficult; his endorsement concerns its direction rather than a ready-made policy.',2706.24,2819.12))
b.append(qa('What would you tell yourself at the beginning?', ['Value the ability to combine skills rather than feel inferior because friends excel more deeply in individual areas. Early uncertainty about his own broad but uneven competence eventually gave way to recognizing its usefulness.', 'He would also be braver and filter himself less. Public speaking once terrified him; pitching a product he cared about felt entirely different from performing knowledge for a class. Passion changed how he engaged. He could not have predicted that communication would later become a large part of his job.'],2820.48,2973.76))
b.append({'type':'flow','title':'A tour of duty changes with the problem','steps':[{'title':'Find the path','text':'Investigate and define an uncertain problem as an IC.'},{'title':'Build the team','text':'Recruit and organize when execution exceeds one person.'},{'title':'Make progress durable','text':'Solve the problem or establish a self-sufficient direction.'},{'title':'Plan succession','text':'Remove dependence on the original pathfinder.'},{'title':'Return to exploration','text':'Choose the next contribution and the role it requires.'}],'caption':'Editorial synthesis. Arrows show a possible project lifecycle in Grigorik’s account, not a required promotion ladder.','editorial':True,'evidence':evidence(1235,1623.04)})
save('source-e0ba6d1b172ce120','Let the problem choose the role','Ilya Grigorik traces a founder, manager, and principal-engineer career built around curiosity, self-sufficient teams, and a versatile combination of skills.',b,'career',['Opening trailer at 0:00–0:41 repeats later exchanges and is represented once.','Closing greetings and podcast support requests at approximately 49:33–50:26 omitted.'],'All five chunks read. Preserves the initiating principal/distinguished question as distinct from its follow-up philosophy, and the separate clarification round. Acquisition observations describe this deal, not legal guidance. Short companion has its own section; repeated material is not independent corroboration.')
